## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable data lifecycle with multi-tenant governance and cost control; integrates Iceberg features, storage tiering, policy-driven retention, and query-layer masking.

## Key Concepts
- Iceberg time travel via snapshots
- Dynamic data tiering (hot/warm/cold)
- Per-tenant data masking
- Encryption at rest (KMS)
- Partition pruning and metadata caching

## Code Example
```javascript
function selectTier(tenant, ageDays) {
  const retention = (tenantRetention[tenant] || 365);
  if (ageDays < 30) return 'hot';
  if (ageDays < retention) return 'warm';
  return 'cold';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data freshness and mask correctness across tiers?
- How would you validate time travel remains accurate after tiering moves?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design multi-tenant data contracts, runtime validation, privacy masking, and governance in a real-time setting. Also probes trade-offs between Spark and Flink, plus testing strategies and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Runtime data contracts per tenant
- Per-tenant masking/redaction strategies
- GDPR deletion and data sovereignty
- Schema evolution in lakehouse (Iceberg/Parquet)
- Policy engine governance (e.g., OPA)
- Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink trade-offs
- Testing, monitoring, data quality

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo runtime contract check
function validateRecord(record, contract) {
  for (const field of Object.keys(contract.fields)) {
    if (!record.hasOwnProperty(field) && contract.fields[field].required) {
      throw new Error(`Missing required field ${field}`);
    }
  }
  // redaction example
  if (contract.mask) {
    for (const f of contract.mask) {
      if (record[f]) record[f] = "REDACTED";
    }
  }
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test per-tenant masking guarantees in CI/CD?
- How would GDPR deletion requests propagate through streaming pipelines and downstream consumers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of batch ETL design with simple data contracts and observable bad data handling.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts and simple validation
- Canonical schema and Parquet lake storage
- Bad data routing with error codes
- Schema drift awareness and basic tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-batch ETL skeleton (Node.js) - not actual Spark
function transform(record) {
  const isValid = record.user_id && record.event_ts && ["click","view","purchase"].includes(record.action);
  if (!isValid) return { bad: true, error_code: "SCHEMA_VIOLATION" };
  return {
    user_id: String(record.user_id),
    event_ts: new Date(record.event_ts).toISOString(),
    action: record.action,
    context: record.context || {}
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the ETL with a tiny dataset and verify bad_data counts?
- How would you extend to handle additional fields while preserving the canonical schema?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?
